# Line endings are decided here, not by whatever core.autocrlf a checkout has.
#
# Why this file exists
# --------------------
# Every checkout of this repository on the Windows development machine carries
# core.autocrlf=true, while .prettierrc.json sets "endOfLine": "lf". The two
# disagree, and the disagreement has cost real time:
#   * a clean `git merge` writes its merged files back out as CRLF, so
#     `pnpm format:check` fails straight afterwards on files nobody edited, and
#     each merge had to be repaired by hand with `git show :<path> > <path>`;
#   * a fresh `git worktree add` off master comes out with every tracked text
#     file CRLF in the working tree (671 of them on 2026-09-12), so the format
#     gate is red before a single line of work is done;
#   * a checkout in that state reports files as modified whose content is
#     byte-identical to the index, and `git diff` prints nothing to explain it,
#     which leaves `git status` with almost no signal;
#   * reports acquired a standing "format:check fails at base, not my doing"
#     exception, which is exactly the note that hides a real failure later.
#
# Attributes act at check-in and checkout. They do NOT renormalise a working
# tree that already exists: an existing checkout still needs the
# one-time `git add --renormalize` sequence recorded in the branch report
# (x4-report.md) and in the implementation-status entry for this change.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Baseline
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# `text=auto` leaves text-vs-binary to Git's content heuristic (binary if a NUL
# byte appears in the first 8000 bytes); `eol=lf` then pins the working-tree
# ending for everything judged text, overriding core.autocrlf and core.eol.
# Since Git 2.10 `eol` no longer defeats that detection, so binaries survive
# this line — but a heuristic is still a heuristic, so every binary format
# actually present here is named explicitly below rather than trusted to it.
#
# What this line does not do: it does not renormalise history or an existing
# working tree, does not add a missing final newline, does not touch trailing
# whitespace, and says nothing about diff/merge treatment of binaries.
* text=auto eol=lf

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Text, named explicitly
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Everything Prettier, ESLint or tsc reads. These are redundant with the
# baseline while detection behaves; they exist so that a file which happens to
# contain an early NUL byte (a fixture, a generated blob) cannot silently be
# reclassified and checked out with CRLF.
*.ts    text eol=lf
*.tsx   text eol=lf
*.js    text eol=lf
*.mjs   text eol=lf
*.cjs   text eol=lf
*.json  text eol=lf
*.css   text eol=lf
*.html  text eol=lf
*.md    text eol=lf
*.yml   text eol=lf
*.yaml  text eol=lf
*.svg   text eol=lf
*.toml  text eol=lf
*.txt   text eol=lf
*.dbml  text eol=lf
.env.example text eol=lf

# Dot-files that would otherwise fall through to the baseline's content check.
.gitattributes  text eol=lf
.gitignore      text eol=lf
.gitmodules     text eol=lf
.prettierignore text eol=lf
.editorconfig   text eol=lf
.npmrc          text eol=lf
.nvmrc          text eol=lf

# SQL migrations. apps/api/src/common/database/migrator.ts already collapses
# \r\n to \n before hashing, so pinning LF cannot invalidate a checksum already
# recorded in schema_migrations. The rule is here to stop an editor committing a
# CRLF blob that then reads as a change to an applied migration.
*.sql text eol=lf

# pnpm writes the lockfile with LF and rewrites it wholesale when it finds CRLF.
# The lockfile is listed in .prettierignore, so the format gate would never
# catch that churn — this is the only place it can be pinned.
pnpm-lock.yaml text eol=lf

# Shell scripts must be LF: with CRLF the kernel reads the shebang as
# `/usr/bin/env bash\r` and fails with "bad interpreter". None are tracked today
# (checked 2026-09-12); the rule is here so the first one added is correct.
*.sh   text eol=lf
*.bash text eol=lf

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Text that genuinely needs CRLF
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# cmd.exe mis-parses labels and GOTO in a batch file with bare LF endings, so
# these are the one exception to the LF rule. Checked on 2026-09-12: this
# repository tracks no .bat and no .cmd, so these lines change nothing today and
# protect the first one committed.
*.bat text eol=crlf
*.cmd text eol=crlf

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Binary — never converted, never diffed, never merged
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# `binary` is Git's built-in macro for `-text -diff -merge`. Enumerated from
# `git ls-files` on 2026-09-12 rather than left to detection: 108 .png (largely
# the Storybook visual-regression baselines, where a single converted byte would
# fail every VR shot), 24 .ttf, 12 .woff2, 12 .woff, 12 .eot, 10 .otf, 3 .webp,
# 3 .jpg, and one WebP file whose name is nothing but an extension.
*.png   binary
*.jpg   binary
*.jpeg  binary
*.webp  binary
*.ttf   binary
*.otf   binary
*.woff  binary
*.woff2 binary
*.eot   binary
docs/design/source/project/.thumbnail binary

# Not tracked today (checked 2026-09-12), listed because each is a format a
# single CRLF substitution corrupts and none should ever wait on someone
# noticing after the fact.
*.ico   binary
*.gif   binary
*.avif  binary
*.pdf   binary
*.zip   binary
*.gz    binary
*.tgz   binary
*.7z    binary
*.mp4   binary
*.mp3   binary
*.wav   binary
*.wasm  binary
*.node  binary
*.xlsx  binary
*.docx  binary
*.pptx  binary

# Patch files carry their own line endings as payload; converting them makes
# `git apply` reject the hunk. Not tracked today, but they are produced by hand
# during merges here.
*.patch -text
*.diff  -text
